The report aims to solve the problem of facial emotion recognition of pets, predict and identify their corresponding expressions and classify them, and divide them into three main emotions: happy, sad and angry. Although the original data set contains a variety of organisms, in the end, only two species of cats and dogs are retained as the main analysis. The facial features of cats and dogs are less different from other creatures, such as "tortoises, hamsters, birds, sheep and horses", and they can provide relatively obvious expression clues. In particular, the changes in the facial features of birds and turtles are difficult to present through static pictures. In contrast, cats and dogs are closer to human life, and their facial features can provide more expression clues, so the scope of research will focus on cats and dogs to reduce species differences. This can help the model focus on learning about expressions.

The core question of the study is whether the facial photos of pets have enough information to accurately distinguish between different expression classifications in the case of cats and dogs. The study will use CNN as the main modeling, using the features of the image to identify different expressions, and using ResNet for in-depth analysis on this basis, so that it is easier to capture the differences in animal expressions. In addition, we also consider using the logistics regression model to compare with the previous model. Through these methods, we hope to discuss whether the pet's face has enough features to be captured and classified by the model, and the feedback of different models in this training.
